[[:db/retract 17592186152483 :question/answer-fields 17592186149472] [:db/retract 17592186152486 :question/answer-fields 17592186149485] {:db/id 17592186152090 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99665861-b940-5f61-80d5-9c97648d3f5e" "question-40d1d6db-2cfa-5253-ab0a-58b0f4105c36" "question-82ba7d3c-5902-5665-8148-515fc5c0a008" "question-eca33d65-a659-5a03-8cda-7de1fade1db7" "question-11d5fb97-c633-54cb-abf0-947c2b0b0a09"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151856 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $3x \\equiv 1 \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)?$ \n\n- $x  \\equiv 5 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 7 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$\n\n- $x  \\equiv 9 \\quad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ we have the following:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $3,$ and $7\\cdot 3 = 21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 7\\cdot 3x & ≡7\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3x \\equiv 1  \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad \nx \\equiv 7\\qquad  (\\textrm{mod}\\,20)\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152091 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e40b6e95-46c9-501b-9cf3-a3ca43d4de46" "question-6f365d70-97c4-5d2a-be4d-f7de85215156" "question-4a7e58ae-7402-5947-902d-187649afe03f" "question-33e605f0-847a-5675-b130-592bda56586a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151840 :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $76 \\cdot 122 \\textrm{ mod } 6.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76 & ≡12\\cdot 6+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡12\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ 122 & ≡20\\cdot 6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡20\\cdot 0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}76\\cdot 122 & ≡4\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡6+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡0+2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n76 \\cdot 122 \\equiv 2 \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,6).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152092 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d32d1e8e-c6cb-5e8e-ad44-72ef38726d73" "question-d8e0aa45-c7f0-5602-aeab-6e88c994c623" "question-cc39a9be-d29f-5f9f-ba0f-0e6107657cab" "question-ca2a2b4b-a4c2-5e49-b032-7cf6b84669a2" "question-a3bd2c71-c0a1-5bcf-99be-f98d89cf7b38"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151841 :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the residue of $5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8.$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n5^{7} = 5^2\\cdot 5^2 \\cdot 5^3.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2} & ≡25\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 8+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡3\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\ {5}^{3} & ≡5\\cdot {5}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{7} & ≡{5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8) \\\\  & ≡5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n5^{7} \\textrm{ mod } 8= 5.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152093 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f6da8c7-058a-5fb2-9ce1-1161fee7e94e" "question-42a09307-b06f-57dd-a795-e7f9c01844a8" "question-b06970b0-8be9-550a-81a5-76772304b000" "question-838b9afb-8969-5aff-9073-fc6f44eddea1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151864 :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of $17^{13}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a\\equiv b\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c\\equiv d\\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac\\equiv bd  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a^k \\equiv b^k  \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17 & ≡10+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{2} & ≡{7}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡49\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 10+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\cdot 0+9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {17}^{4} & ≡{9}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose $17^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n17^{13} =  \\left(17^4\\right)^3\\cdot 17\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{17}^{13} & ≡{({17}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 17\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡7\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n17^{13} \\textrm{ mod } 10 = 7\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of $17^{13}$ is $7.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-99665861-b940-5f61-80d5-9c97648d3f5e" :question/id #uuid "99665861-b940-5f61-80d5-9c97648d3f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213133" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $2x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)?$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $15$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $2,$ and $8\\cdot 2=16$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 8\\cdot 2x & ≡8\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ 16x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+15x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x+0x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15) \\\\ x & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04c27edf-55f3-5d46-8580-2a951a40bef4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04c27edf-55f3-5d46-8580-2a951a40bef4" :answer-field/id #uuid "04c27edf-55f3-5d46-8580-2a951a40bef4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82e0f3af-6b97-591d-9ecb-884c1849cb82" "answer-7f141946-1c43-5094-b193-1606569a4874" "answer-cb288acd-51c3-5990-bbed-76cf3f8b1472" "answer-20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" "answer-f047ef3d-27cd-555d-bd60-7dcb9abcab96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82e0f3af-6b97-591d-9ecb-884c1849cb82" :answer/id #uuid "82e0f3af-6b97-591d-9ecb-884c1849cb82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡12\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f141946-1c43-5094-b193-1606569a4874" :answer/id #uuid "7f141946-1c43-5094-b193-1606569a4874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb288acd-51c3-5990-bbed-76cf3f8b1472" :answer/id #uuid "cb288acd-51c3-5990-bbed-76cf3f8b1472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" :answer/id #uuid "20a1677a-5c04-5a45-a564-8c69e8a4b6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡8\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f047ef3d-27cd-555d-bd60-7dcb9abcab96" :answer/id #uuid "f047ef3d-27cd-555d-bd60-7dcb9abcab96" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,15)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-40d1d6db-2cfa-5253-ab0a-58b0f4105c36" :question/id #uuid "40d1d6db-2cfa-5253-ab0a-58b0f4105c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-213139" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $7x≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)?$" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nThe modulus equals $20$ in all statements, which suggests we'll need to use the first property.\n\nNotice that the coefficient of $x$ is $7,$ and $3\\cdot 7=21$ is one larger than the modulus. Therefore, we can reduce the coefficient of $x$ to unity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 3\\cdot 7x & ≡3\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ 21x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+20x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x+0x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20) \\\\ x & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,20)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7775cb9-ebf4-544c-952d-3118f995104e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7775cb9-ebf4-544c-952d-3118f995104e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7775cb9-ebf4-544c-952d-3118f995104e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a9cb5e3-85f6-561e-9093-c94e6d9abf72" "answer-22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" "answer-0ff8646e-75ee-56e3-9225-450328cee04d" "answer-e435e15c-46e8-54cf-b47a-2035d47492ea" "answer-e2d94439-6cba-5374-b5a5-caa715e4b5b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a9cb5e3-85f6-561e-9093-c94e6d9abf72" :answer/id #uuid "6a9cb5e3-85f6-561e-9093-c94e6d9abf72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" :answer/id #uuid "22d2c81b-0b19-5496-9c41-4e747d1da9df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,20)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ff8646e-75ee-56e3-9225-450328cee04d" :answer/id #uuid "0