:question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52db949a-3bc2-5457-9b45-0541c374fc01"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52db949a-3bc2-5457-9b45-0541c374fc01" :answer-field/id #uuid "52db949a-3bc2-5457-9b45-0541c374fc01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-87d21540-9254-5db6-a797-2a68b4857675" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87d21540-9254-5db6-a797-2a68b4857675" "answer-db5ab6f3-81eb-55ce-a699-76779a629184" "answer-4c67fae5-e78b-5a53-afbb-dc8f4157890f" "answer-60453212-d637-59fa-b5b9-ef41bcd4295e" "answer-eb0e066c-8da6-5a88-8bc6-6b8dc06280b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87d21540-9254-5db6-a797-2a68b4857675" :answer/id #uuid "87d21540-9254-5db6-a797-2a68b4857675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db5ab6f3-81eb-55ce-a699-76779a629184" :answer/id #uuid "db5ab6f3-81eb-55ce-a699-76779a629184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c67fae5-e78b-5a53-afbb-dc8f4157890f" :answer/id #uuid "4c67fae5-e78b-5a53-afbb-dc8f4157890f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60453212-d637-59fa-b5b9-ef41bcd4295e" :answer/id #uuid "60453212-d637-59fa-b5b9-ef41bcd4295e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb0e066c-8da6-5a88-8bc6-6b8dc06280b2" :answer/id #uuid "eb0e066c-8da6-5a88-8bc6-6b8dc06280b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3bd2c71-c0a1-5bcf-99be-f98d89cf7b38" :question/id #uuid "a3bd2c71-c0a1-5bcf-99be-f98d89cf7b38" :question/math-academy-id "q-213071" :question/problem "Use the multiplicative property of congruences to compute the following residue.\n\n${8}^{6}\\text{ mod }9=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicative property of modular congruences states that if\n\n$$\na≡b\\,\\text{and}\\,c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nFirst, we decompose the exponent into a product of smaller exponents. For example,\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}={8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{2} & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 9+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡7\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the multiplicative property of congruences, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{6} & ≡{8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 1\\cdot 1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{8}^{6}\\,\\,\\text{mod}\\,9=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52eefa79-7a00-5342-a326-3242d4e53917"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52eefa79-7a00-5342-a326-3242d4e53917" :answer-field/id #uuid "52eefa79-7a00-5342-a326-3242d4e53917" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83" :answer/id #uuid "5ecf2566-0fbb-5c3c-bd2b-a10c6c13ce83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6f6da8c7-058a-5fb2-9ce1-1161fee7e94e" :question/id #uuid "6f6da8c7-058a-5fb2-9ce1-1161fee7e94e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213171" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${28}^{18}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & ≡20+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 10+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡2\\cdot 0+8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{2} & ≡{8}^{2}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ {28}^{6} & ≡{({28}^{2})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡{4}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${28}^{18}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{28}^{18}={({28}^{6})}^{3}\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{28}^{18} & ≡{({28}^{6})}^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(4)^{3}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡64\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 10+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡6\\cdot 0+4 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{28}^{18}\\text{ mod }10=4\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${28}^{18}$ is $4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b24580b0-549e-52ef-8518-91948f4e984f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b24580b0-549e-52ef-8518-91948f4e984f" :answer-field/id #uuid "b24580b0-549e-52ef-8518-91948f4e984f" :answer-field/key "selection" :ans