 "answer-field-7e6e82e0-cc26-55f9-90de-b2b59a403f09" :answer-field/id #uuid "7e6e82e0-cc26-55f9-90de-b2b59a403f09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-06cf77a2-4eb2-52c0-9c0d-e1645c260e8e" "answer-b2fd01be-dff1-54f7-8430-1207d43687c5" "answer-f7893b80-75f3-5c3b-9f72-1ca9d9f6c35a" "answer-0d761cac-98d1-57a4-961b-d01d89bb8dd7" "answer-dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06cf77a2-4eb2-52c0-9c0d-e1645c260e8e" :answer/id #uuid "06cf77a2-4eb2-52c0-9c0d-e1645c260e8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2fd01be-dff1-54f7-8430-1207d43687c5" :answer/id #uuid "b2fd01be-dff1-54f7-8430-1207d43687c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7893b80-75f3-5c3b-9f72-1ca9d9f6c35a" :answer/id #uuid "f7893b80-75f3-5c3b-9f72-1ca9d9f6c35a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d761cac-98d1-57a4-961b-d01d89bb8dd7" :answer/id #uuid "0d761cac-98d1-57a4-961b-d01d89bb8dd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5" :answer/id #uuid "dbe624ca-e41b-54cb-83a5-2c4bec9e21b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-838b9afb-8969-5aff-9073-fc6f44eddea1" :question/id #uuid "838b9afb-8969-5aff-9073-fc6f44eddea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213181" :question/problem "What is the last digit in the decimal expansion of ${13}^{13}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ and $c≡d\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n),$ then the following properties hold:\n\n- $ac≡bd\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- ${a}^{k}≡{b}^{k}\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ for any positive integer $k$\n\nTo find the last digit, we need to calculate the following residue:\n\n$$\n{13}^{13}\\text{ mod }10\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & ≡10+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the exponentiation property, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{13}^{4} & ≡{3}^{4}\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡81\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 10+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡8\\cdot 0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we decompose ${13}^{13}$ into a product of smaller exponents. \n\n$$\n{13}^{13}={({13}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 13\n$$\n\nBy applying the multiplicative and exponentiation properties, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{13}^{13} & ≡{({13}^{4})}^{3}\\cdot 13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡(1)^{3}\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡1\\cdot 3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\  & ≡3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{13}^{13}\\text{ mod }10=3\n$$\n\nwhich means that the last digit of ${13}^{13}$ is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-56c4a31d-bd95-5f8c-8294-722cc10f2925"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-56c4a31d-bd95-5f8c-8294-722cc10f2925" :answer-field/id #uuid "56c4a31d-bd95-5f8c-8294-722cc10f2925" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb8c4405-00c9-59d2-ba23-c4c7852c42d1" "answer-3b80a56e-c829-5e05-9c76-7a0b2e001220" "answer-b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" "answer-b82a41c9-3f94-5a68-a88b-e972b19da354" "answer-c283465c-37c3-5dcb-9033-b8f74eb40b53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb8c4405-00c9-59d2-ba23-c4c7852c42d1" :answer/id #uuid "fb8c4405-00c9-59d2-ba23-c4c7852c42d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b80a56e-c829-5e05-9c76-7a0b2e001220" :answer/id #uuid "3b80a56e-c829-5e05-9c76-7a0b2e001220" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" :answer/id #uuid "b910e2e2-943c-5104-b1ad-704aa8711025" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82a41c9-3f94-5a68-a88b-e972b19da354" :answer/id #uuid "b82a41c9-3f94-5a68-a88b-e972b19da354" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c283465c-37c3-5dcb-9033-b8f74eb40b53" :answer/id #uuid "c283465c-37c3-5dcb-9033-b8f74eb40b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eca33d65-a659-5a03-8cda-7de1fade1db7" :question/id #uuid "eca33d65-a659-5a03-8cda-7de1fade1db7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213124" :question/problem "Given that $u≡v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nSince the modulus is unchanged in both statements, let's multiply by $5$ and apply the first property.\n\n$$\nu≡v\\,⟹\\,5u≡5v\\,(\\text{mod}\\,12)\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4db3cfdb-b728-56df-9b15-86079fd51bef" "answer-field-427f6584-c0af-52e4-81d5-6532a479ad5b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4db3cfdb-b728-56df-9b15-86079fd51bef" :answer-field/id #uuid "4db3cfdb-b728-56df-9b15-86079fd51bef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-57cd1c6c-0479-553c-a784-a40c9ffd8e91" "answer-14b8aea0-1d18-58fa-bfdd-4c182e291f7e" "answer-b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57cd1c6c-0479-553c-a784-a40c9ffd8e91" :answer/id #uuid "57cd1c6c-0479-553c-a784-a40c9ffd8e91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14b8aea0-1d18-58fa-bfdd-4c182e291f7e" :answer/id #uuid "14b8aea0-1d18-58fa-bfdd-4c182e291f7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3" :answer/id #uuid "b32bab38-80c0-58ee-aa2a-f14d8389a1d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-427f6584-c0af-52e4-81d5-6532a479ad5b" :answer-field/id #uuid "427f6584-c0af-52e4-81d5-6532a479ad5b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a606d71f-4abf-56b6-b723-489fde7cf778" "answer-b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" "answer-1eac1284-7e8a-5cd8-9b01-790d6397d4ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a606d71f-4abf-56b6-b723-489fde7cf778" :answer/id #uuid "a606d71f-4abf-56b6-b723-489fde7cf778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" :answer/id #uuid "b65606a0-5323-5b38-88b9-565e502ff7dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1eac1284-7e8a-5cd8-9b01-790d6397d4ed" :answer/id #uuid "1eac1284-7e8a-5cd8-9b01-790d6397d4ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11d5fb97-c633-54cb-abf0-947c2b0b0a09" :question/id #uuid "11d5fb97-c633-54cb-abf0-947c2b0b0a09" :question/math-academy-id "q-213175" :question/problem "Given that $w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$ construct a statement that also must be true:" :question/worked-solution "The scalar multiplication properties of congruences state that for $k\\in Z,$ we have the following:\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's multiply our congruence by $2$ and apply the second property.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2\\cdot w≡2\\cdot z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2\\cdot 8) \\\\ w≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)\\, & ⟹\\,2w≡2z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-90300d85-4a51-50a2-a299-6e653a1cb36c" "answer-field-ccdccd76-2c21-545a-987d-d111b3a32d9e" "answer-field-c4b3178a-a2ad-5e3b-87c5-69822d78d4d6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-90300d85-4a51-50a2-a299-6e653a1cb36c" :answer-field/id #uuid "90300d85-4a51-50a2-a299-6e653a1cb36c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" "answer-98ee589d-41dd-514c-9baf-76daf28eb959" "answer-eb74c9c6-a30e-5a54-8b11-c5961ee030c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" :answer/id #uuid "e8dabb8c-6524-5da7-a90b-c3cd126d21e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98ee589d-41dd-514c-9baf-76daf28eb959" :answer/id #uuid "98ee589d-41dd-514c-9baf-76daf28eb959" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb74c9c6-a30e-5a54-8b11-c5961ee030c6" :answer/id #uuid "eb74c9c6-a30e-5a54-8b11-c5961ee030c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ccdccd76-2c21-545a-987d-d111b3a32d9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "ccdccd76-2c21-545a-987d-d111b3a32d9e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" "answer-72bf9960-d3d8-5c97-b6ad-3321009b5c6c" "answer-429c0671-53eb-580a-bb20-0838f8c82926"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" :answer/id #uuid "44171d2c-8cd4-581d-9539-b26372a15528" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72bf9960-d3d8-5c97-b6ad-3321009b5c6c" :answer/id #uuid "72bf9960-d3d8-5c97-b6ad-3321009b5c6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-429c0671-53eb-580a-bb20-0838f8c82926" :answer/id #uuid "429c0671-53eb-580a-bb20-0838f8c82926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c4b3178a-a2ad-5e3b-87c5-69822d78d4d6" :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b3178a-a2ad-5e3b-87c5-69822d78d4d6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87839af9-ade8-5f64-a086-270fb3250d17" "answer-4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" "answer-5705870f-d933-531e-a16a-43e901ed4939"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87839af9-ade8-5f64-a086-270fb3250d17" :answer/id #uuid "87839af9-ade8-5f64-a086-270fb3250d17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" :answer/id #uuid "4a68e88c-54bf-5c63-ba79-072dec97003c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5705870f-d933-531e-a16a-43e901ed4939" :answer/id #uuid "5705870f-d933-531e-a16a-43e901ed4939" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153451 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the modular congruence\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nOne useful property of congruences is that we can *multiply* both sides by an *integer*, similar to regular equations.\n\nFor example, consider the true statement\n\n$$\n7\\equiv 2 \\quad (\\textrm{mod}\\,5).\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by (for example) $9$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\cdot 7 & ≡9\\cdot 2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ 63 & ≡18\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement since $63$ and $18$ both have a remainder of $3$ when divided by $5.$\n\nIn general, the **scalar multiplication property** of congruences states that for $k\\in\\mathbb{Z},$\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\na\\equiv b  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n) \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,kn).\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153463 :tutorial/content "Another useful property that stems from the multiplication property is as follows. Suppose that \n\n$$\na\\equiv b \\quad \\textrm{and}\\quad c\\equiv d \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),\n$$\n\nthen, we have \n\n$$\nac\\equiv bd \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nUsing this, we can compute the residue of a large product without finding the product explicitly. Let's see how." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153469 :tutorial/content "The multiplicative property states that given $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$ and $c \\equiv d \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n),$ we obtain $ac \\equiv bd \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).$ By taking the same congruence twice, we obtain \n\n$$\na^2 \\equiv b^2  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\na^k \\equiv b^k  \\qquad (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nfor any *positive* integer $k.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153473 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson, we've made use of the following properties:\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- $a \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,kn)$\n\nLet's prove the first property. The proof of the second property is similar.\n\nIt's straightforward to prove the scalar multiplication property from the definition of congruence. First, note that we have the following chain of implications for $k, p\\in\\mathbb Z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\, & ⟹\\,a-b=pn \\\\  & ⟹\\,k(a-b)=kpn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka-kb=(kp)n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $kp = q\\in\\mathbb Z.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ka-kb=(kp)n\\, & ⟹\\,ka-kb=qn \\\\  & ⟹\\,ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\na \\equiv b \\: (\\textrm{mod}\\,n)  \\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad ka \\equiv kb \\:(\\textrm{mod}\\,n).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The proof of the second property is very similar.\n\n- We'll prove the multiplicative and exponentiation properties in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152341 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
